From d5dad058c669b91dace548270a30b7991d73c1c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 20:49:33 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08071/11362] [outer-loop] name the unregistered-game case
 instead of raising AttributeError

`adapters.get_adapter()` returns None for an unregistered game, and
`_live_verifier_for_adapter` walked straight into `adapter.hand_verifier` on it.
The resulting "AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute
'hand_verifier'" reads like a broken solve rather than a missing registration --
which is why sc25, tn36 and wa30 looked UNSOLVABLE offline when they were merely
UNREGISTERED, and sat outside every A/B corpus built through
build_progress_window until someone read the traceback closely.

Raises NoAdapterForGame(LookupError) naming the game, listing what IS registered,
and pointing at _BUILDERS. A distinct type so a caller sweeping many games can
skip an unregistered one instead of aborting.

Offline dev path only. The scored agent has ZERO adapter references, verified by
import-closure analysis over its 43 carnot modules: 0 calls to get_adapter /
solve_adaptered / adaptered_games, and arc_game_adapters is not in the closure at
all. That is structural, not luck -- a hidden game can never have an adapter, so
any dependency would fail every submission rather than intermittently.

Also lands the adapter-depth work this came out of:

  scripts/arc_adapter_depth_probe.py  MEASURES how deep each adapter really
      solves, one (game, level) per subprocess with a hard timeout so a hang
      degrades to a recorded TIMEOUT instead of wedging the sweep. It does NOT
      parse the registry's prose `solver` field -- regexing "L1-L2" out of free
      text is the pattern-narrower-than-its-concept failure being guarded
      against. The measured number becomes the claim.
  tests/python/test_arc_adapter_depth.py  guards that record without re-solving.

The split is forced by measurement: a single solve_adaptered call exceeds 10
minutes (tr87 L1 took 333s; tr87 L2 timed out at 600s), so a 24-game sweep is a
milestone-close job, not a pytest. First real data: tr87 depth 1, r11l depth 2 --
consistent with lf52's registry prose ("L1-L2: GameAdapter ... L3: <other
mechanism>") and with adapters being a shallow-prefix tool by design.

12 tests. test_wa30_is_the_only_unregistered_public_game pins the current state
so adding or losing an adapter is a deliberate, visible edit.
